snp2.14:20.1 starts: Laying aside violence toward all living creatures,
snp2.14:20.2 starts: both the firm & unfirm in the world,
snp2.14:20.3 starts: one should not kill a living being, nor
snp2.14:20.4 starts: nor condone killing by others.
snp3.1:13.1 starts: Hearing the messenger’s words,
snp3.1:13.2 starts: the noble-warrior king
snp3.1:13.3 starts: straight away set out by royal coach,
snp3.1:13.4 starts: for Mount Paṇḍava.
snp3.2:9.1 starts: This wind could burn up
snp3.2:9.2 starts: even river currents.
snp3.2:9.3 starts: Why, when my mind is resolute,
snp3.2:9.4 starts: shouldn’t my blood dry away?
snp2.14:28.1 starts: Then, having kept, well-accomplished,
snp2.14:28.2 starts: the eightfold uposatha
snp2.14:28.3 starts: on the fourteenth, fifteenth, & eighth
snp2.14:28.4 starts: and on special days of the fortnight,
snp2.14:29.1 starts: the observant person
snp2.14:29.2 starts: at dawn after the uposatha,
snp2.14:29.3 starts: should share food & drink,
snp2.14:29.4 starts: as is proper,
snp3.1:7.1 starts: This one’s not like one
snp3.1:7.2 starts: from a lowly lineage:
snp3.1:7.3 starts: Have the royal messengers hurry
snp3.1:7.4 starts: to see where this monk will go.”
snp3.1:15.1 starts: Sitting there,
snp3.1:15.2 starts: he exchanged courteous greetings,
snp3.1:15.3 starts: and after giving friendly greetings,
snp3.1:15.4 starts: said this:
snp3.2:17.1 starts: Sinking here, they don’t appear,
snp3.2:17.2 starts: some brahmans & contemplatives.
snp3.2:17.3 starts: They don’t know the path
snp3.2:17.4 starts: by which those with good practices
snp3.2:18.1 starts: Seeing the bannered force
snp3.2:18.2 starts: the troops, Māra
snp3.2:18.3 starts: I go into battle.
snp3.2:18.4 starts: May they not budge me
snp3.3:6.1 starts: Speak only endearing speech,
snp3.3:6.2 starts: speech that is welcomed.
snp3.3:6.3 starts: Speech when it brings no evil
snp3.3:6.4 starts: is pleasant.
snp3.4:7.1 starts: The Buddha:
snp3.4:7.2 starts: and I’m not a brahman,
snp3.4:7.3 starts: I ask you the three lines of the
snp3.4:7.4 starts: and its twenty-four syllables.”
